\subsection{Automatic supervision of human activities}
\sidenoteMJ{este apartado esta muy orientado a supervision que creo ya no toca. Yo extraería la parte de activity recognition unicamente}
<<COMMENT_1311>>
<<COMMENT_1312>>
The recognition of complex human activities has been widely addressed by the area of Activity Recognition (AR). AR can be considered the base of the most of supervision systems. In AR, a distinction can be made between data-driven approaches and knowledge-driven approaches following the differentiation provided in <<CMD_1951>>. Data-driven approaches (<<CMD_1952>>, <<CMD_1953>>, <<CMD_1954>>, <<CMD_1955>>) are characterised by using supervised (e.g., Hidden Markov Models (HMM), Linear Dynamical Systems (LDSs)) and unsupervised (e.g., KNN) machine learning techniques to address the problem. These are powerful tools when facing uncertainty and temporal information but they require large datasets for learning the activities. This dependency of data makes data-driven approaches to be sensitive to issues such as data scarcity or even the ``cold start'' problem: the problem that emerges when a data-driven application does not have the minimum amount of data to learn the task and operate. In addition, limitations in scalability and reusability may arise here due to difficulties in applying the learnt activity models from one person to another. Above all, despite being possible to describe a scene by recognising the sequence of actions, in many cases, it is meaningless for understanding the task by itself, so a high-level reasoning is required to those effects. 

By contrast, knowledge-driven approaches (<<CMD_1956>>, <<CMD_1957>>, <<CMD_1958>>, <<CMD_1959>>, <<CMD_1960>>) are characterised by their reusability, semantic clarity and a lower dependency from the training data. Activity models consist of rules that define the logic of the task and its constraints. This representation make it possible the introduction of domain knowledge about tasks to supervise. This prior knowledge can be translated easily into reusable structures, i.e., schemes, logical representation or ontologies, which are then used for reasoning about the relationships between activities, objects, temporal and spatial context. Furthermore, they provide some interesting features, such as deduction, induction, and abduction, that pave the way for the generation of new knowledge from examples or predicting future events using commonsense reasoning. On the other hand, knowledge-driven activity modelling has limitations when handling noisy and uncertain data from sensor readings, as well as managing temporal information. In some cases, these models could be viewed as quite rigid and incomplete <<CMD_1961>>. 

<<COMMENT_1313>>
The applicability of both types of approaches inside a real automatic supervision system has become a hot topic in recent years. On the side of data-based systems, we can find that Markov-based models are predominant. For instance, in <<CMD_1857>> the authors present a data-driven system for assisting persons with dementia during handwashing. The process is modelled by using a \textit{partially observable Markov decision process} (POMDP). This type of model makes it possible to represent the uncertainty control and the user’s mental states by adding rewards and costs to the actions. The results showed that the system is accurate and provides reliable assistance to people with dementia. Kitchen activities supervision is also a hot topic for data-driven approaches. <<CMD_1858>> studied the feasibility of Computational Causal Behaviour Models (CCBM) for providing assistance when the user is cooking the meal. By distributing a realistic sensor setups on the kitchen objects and the human subject (using wearable inertial measurement units or IMUs), the CCBM is not only capable of recognising the current cooking activities, but also the user intentions and goals from noisy sensor data. The topic of supervising cooking tasks through a data-driven approach has also been addressed by <<CMD_1859>>, <<CMD_1860>>, and  <<CMD_1861>>. A wider scope would include also supervision in a whole smart environment using sensor data <<CMD_1962>>, <<CMD_1963>>, <<CMD_1964>>, <<CMD_1965>>.
